<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sangha">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sangha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as saṅgha, saṃgha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sangha is a term meaning association, assembly or community. Historically it denoted the governing assembly of a republic or a kingdom, and it has long been used by religious associations, among them Buddhists, Jains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> it refers above all to the monastic communities of monks (bhikkhu) and nuns (bhikkhuni), established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a> in the fifth century BCE for those who wished to practise full-time, free of household life. Members follow the vinaya, whose 227 main rules include complete chastity and eating only before noon. The sangha is the third of the Three Refuges, and it has preserved, translated and spread the Buddha's teachings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sangha.jpg" alt="Sangha" width="330" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Darrenjsmith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RatoMonks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> the term refers to the monastic communities of bhikkhu (monks) and bhikkhuni (nuns), traditionally called the bhikkhu-sangha and the bhikkhuni-sangha. As a separate category, Buddhists who have attained any of the four stages of enlightenment, whether or not they belong to the monastic community, are called the āryasaṅgha, the noble Sangha. A glossary by Richard Robinson and others gives two levels of meaning: an ideal level, covering all the Buddha's followers, lay or ordained, who have reached at least the level of srotāpanna, and a conventional level, covering the orders of monks and nuns. The two overlap but are not identical, since some members of the ideal Sangha are not ordained.</p><p>Usage differs between schools. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> practitioners may use the word for all Buddhists, whereas the Pāli Canon uses parisā, the assembly, for the larger community of monks, nuns, laymen and laywomen who have taken the Three Refuges. The Sangha is the third of those refuges, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> tradition its qualities are chanted daily or on Uposatha days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Sangha was established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a> in the fifth century BCE for those who wished to practise full-time, free from the responsibilities of household life. It has also preserved the Buddha's teachings, given spiritual support to the lay community, and taken responsibility for the integrity of the doctrine and for its translation and propagation. Its key feature is adherence to the vinaya, which contains 227 main rules of conduct, known as the Patimokkha in Pāli, including complete chastity and eating only before noon. Transgressions carry penalties from confession to permanent expulsion.</p><p>The words bhikkhu and bhikkhuni mean one who lives by alms, and early monastics went on daily alms rounds. The vinaya did not allow the sangha to farm, but in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> monastic communities came to engage in agriculture, an emphasis attributed to the Chan master Baizhang Huaihai. In Japan Saichō, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a>, reduced the rules to about 60, based on the Bodhisattva Precepts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In its political sense the word denoted a governing assembly in a republic or a kingdom, and some Buddhists have called the sangha humanity's oldest surviving democratic institution. Nichiren Shōshū holds that the sangha is the priesthood of its head temple Taisekiji, while the Soka Gakkai, expelled from it in December 1991, understands the sangha to include all who practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in its way, lay or clerical.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sangha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For some forty-five years he taught across the Ganges valley, gathering a community, the <b>Sangha</b>, of monks and later nuns, supported by lay followers who included merchants and kings.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sri Lankan chronicle Mahāvaṃsa records that it was first written down in the 1st century BCE, ending a tradition of oral preservation, because famine, war and the rival Abhayagiri Vihāra threatened the <b>Sangha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/pali">Pali · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Minor Rock Edicts and Minor Pillar Edicts are very religious: they mention the Buddha, the <b>Sangha</b> and Buddhist scriptures, and Minor Rock Edict No.</blockquote><a href="/work/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Men and women entered the clergy by taking vows, and service to the court could lead far: <b>Sangha</b>, who rose through the Buddhist bureau, became the dynasty’s chief fiscal officer in 1286.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
